One reasonable approach is as follows. Currently Field_1 and Field_2 are fully covered (with 4 drones each) but Fields 3 and 4 – especially Field_4 with a threat level of 0.10 – are left unprotected. Since only eight drones are available and full protection is only achieved when the field gets the required number of drones, it can be advantageous to “reallocate” some of the drones from the less‐critical Field_1 (threat 0.09) to the more–critical Field_4 (which needs 3 drones for full coverage). 

In the assignment above the four drones on Field_2 remain in place because Field_2 has the highest threat (0.17). Out of the four protecting Field_1, three are reassigned to Field_4 (making that field fully protected), while one is left on Field_1 (providing some—if not full— protection, which reduces the damage compared to leaving it completely vulnerable). Field_3, which has a low threat level (0.03), is left without protection.

This assignment therefore covers the two most urgent fields (Field_2 and Field_4) and reduces the overall potential damage.
